 1 big thing — Axios interview: GOP&#x27;s midterm villain 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Axios' Alex Thompson interviews Hasan Piker in his L.A. home yesterday. Photo: Michael Rossetti for Axios Hasan Piker told the media briefing Status that yesterday's "The Axios Show" taping with Alex Thompson was "the most contentious interview I've ever conducted." 

 LOS ANGELES — In a new episode of " The Axios Show ," out tomorrow, left-wing streamer Hasan Piker says he believes America conducts more terrorism in the world than al-Qaeda.

 "Pound for pound as far as the number of deaths, yes," Piker told Thompson. He added: "We have caused tremendous terror all around the world." Why it matters: Piker is a top streamer on Twitch, a platform popular with gamers. His history of incendiary statements has become central to Republicans' attacks in Michigan's critical Senate race, after Piker campaigned for Democratic nominee Abdul El-Sayed in the primary.

 🔎 Zoom in: Piker's comments to Axios came in response to a question about his previous statement that "America is the top dog in terrorism."

 Piker pointed to the deaths of more than 100 people at an elementary school in Minab, Iran, earlier this year. AP and The New York Times found evidence that at least one U.S. missile struck the school. "If you're killing 140 schoolgirls, that is an act of terror," he said. "This doesn't mean that all Americans are terrorists. But the reality of the matter is we have to create an environment of accountability to ensure things like that never happen again." 🖼️ The big picture: Republican-affiliated groups have already spent nearly $3 million in ads attacking El-Sayed over his affiliation with Piker, according to the tracking firm AdImpact.

 Most of the ads feature a clip of Piker in 2019 saying "America deserved 9/11." He apologized for the remark and has since argued that the terror attacks were part of "blowback" from U.S. involvement in the Middle East in previous decades. "This doesn't justify the action, mind you," he told Axios. "This is more so analysis to stop further violence from taking place, both violence in the region, but also violence on the home front as well." Michigan's GOP Senate nominee, Mike Rogers, frequently ties Piker to El-Sayed.

 El-Sayed has dodged questions about Piker and occasionally appeared irritated by them, often referring to him as just a "streamer in California." In a statement last night, El-Sayed said: "I do not agree with this statement [on al-Qaeda], nor do I give much weight to the statements made by a social media streamer." ▶️ Watch a 1-min. clip . 

 
 
