 3. 💊 AI&#x27;s $40 billion drug gamble 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Curing disease is one of AI's most tantalizing use cases, attracting tens of billions in venture funding to design promising new drugs. Proving they actually work in humans is the hard part, Axios' Dan Primack and Caitlin Owens write. 

 Why it matters: AI is getting better at identifying drug candidates, but they still have to survive the same expensive, failure-prone clinical trials as everything else. 🖼️ The big picture: A peer-reviewed paper published in Nature Reviews Drug Discovery argues that AI's clinical impact remains "disappointingly limited," despite major advances in identifying potential drugs.

 The researchers find that while AI has gotten good at identifying drug candidates, it hasn't done much to prove those predictions will survive the complexity of human biology. AI-derived drugs still run into the same bottleneck as conventional drugs: proving efficacy in large, diverse patient populations, where failure rates are high and costs are astronomical. 🧮 By the numbers: Venture capitalists have poured more than $40 billion into AI biopharma this decade, according to PitchBook.

 But few AI-derived medicines have progressed beyond Phase II clinical trials, where researchers test whether a drug actually works in patients with the disease. At the same time, the FDA is trying to shave time off an earlier part of the drug-development process. 

 A new pilot will let drugmakers submit applications on a rolling basis, aiming to move promising drug candidates from discovery into human clinical trials faster. The push reflects a broader concern that the U.S. drug-development system is too slow, particularly as early-stage clinical development can move faster and more cheaply in China. Share this story . 

 
 
